# Facts about building Lyra, and the definition of each opt-in `--config`.
# Nothing here requires an account, a credential, or a particular machine:
# `git clone` then `bazel build //...` works for anyone. Machine tuning and
# credentials go in `.bazelrc.user`, git-ignored and imported at the bottom.

# Which compiler builds Lyra and where its actions run are separate choices.
# The compiler is the clang `MODULE.bazel` pins, unless `gcc` asks for the
# remote image's GCC, the one GCC Lyra is held to. That GCC exists only in the
# image, so `gcc` runs the actions there and needs a key as `rbe` does. Where
# the actions run is otherwise this machine, unless `rbe` sends them to
# BuildBuddy.
build:gcc --config=rbe
build:gcc --extra_toolchains=@buildbuddy_toolchain//:ubuntu_cc_toolchain

build --cxxopt='-std=c++23'

# LLVM is optimized even in an unoptimized build, and nothing else is. The
# conformance corpus runs the compiler once per case, and almost all of that
# time is spent inside LLVM producing machine code: at the default compilation
# mode a case costs around ten times what it costs with LLVM optimized, which
# is the whole of why that suite is measured in minutes.
#
# Scoped to LLVM because the two sides have opposite cost profiles. Lyra's own
# sources are edited constantly and lowering a design through them is a tenth
# of a second either way, so they stay unoptimized and the edit loop is
# unchanged. LLVM is third-party, rebuilds only when its pin moves, and the
# result is shared through the cache, so the build cost is paid once rather
# than per edit.
#
# Assertions are deliberately left on: `-DNDEBUG` would buy a little more speed
# by disabling the checks LLVM makes on what we hand it, which are the ones
# that catch our own misuse of it.
#
# The pattern matches the repository's directory by the one part of its name
# that is LLVM's own. Bazel prefixes the directory with the module that provides
# the repository, so a pattern spelling the whole name matches only the module it
# was written against and silently leaves LLVM unoptimized under another one.
build --per_file_copt=external/[^/]*llvm-project[^/]*/.*@-O2

# LLVM is built without zlib. Its build reaches zlib-ng, which defines every
# zlib function under zlib's own names, while the value dump's FST writer is
# built on zlib itself, so a program linking both holds two definitions of
# each and which one a call reaches depends on the link. Nothing Lyra asks of
# LLVM compresses, so the one zlib in a link is the dump's.
build --@llvm-project//third-party:llvm_enable_zlib=false

build --incompatible_strict_action_env
build --verbose_failures

# What a build prints is Lyra's own: a third-party repository's warnings are
# not ours to act on, and LLVM alone prints hundreds whenever it is compiled.
# This filters what is shown, not how anything is compiled, so it moves no
# cache key; a failing action is still reported whichever repository owns it.
build --output_filter=^//

test --test_output=errors

# The default set is what the merge gate runs, so a local run answers "does this
# land green" without naming anything. It leaves out the targets whose cost no
# merge-time budget holds; `tests/BUILD.bazel` states which and why.
#
# A filtered run reports only the tests it ran and nothing names what was left
# out, so a result that has to stand as evidence uses `--config=full`. A change
# to what the C++ backend emits needs it too: those targets are the only ones
# compiling emitted text, and a green default run says nothing about it.
test --test_tag_filters=-nightly
test:full --test_tag_filters=
test:nightly --test_tag_filters=nightly

# A run someone is waiting on stops at the first case that fails, because a
# defect the whole corpus shares and a defect one case has look identical from
# outside while the run is still going, and only the first is worth waiting
# through. Someone waiting is the common case, so it is the default.
#
# What a scheduled run produces instead is the list of which cases fail, and a
# list that ends at its first entry is not one -- so the two sets a schedule
# draws on say so, and the gate, which names no config, says it in the untracked
# layer it already writes. `full` is not among them: it is a developer asking
# for everything, and they are still waiting. None of this changes which tests
# are selected, so a local command and the gate still answer the same question.
test --test_arg=--gtest_fail_fast
test:nightly --test_arg=--gtest_fail_fast=false
test:asan --test_arg=--gtest_fail_fast=false

build:debug --compilation_mode=dbg

build:release --compilation_mode=opt
build:release --cxxopt='-O3'
build:release --cxxopt='-DNDEBUG'

build:fastbuild --compilation_mode=fastbuild
build:fastbuild --cxxopt='-O1'

# Address sanitizer, for the nightly that runs the merge gate's own set under
# it. A read of memory that is gone usually reads what was there, so an
# ordinary run is green either way; under the sanitizer the read is reported.
# `-O1` and a kept frame pointer are what make the report name the read.
build:asan --copt='-fsanitize=address'
build:asan --copt='-fno-omit-frame-pointer'
build:asan --copt='-O1'
build:asan --linkopt='-fsanitize=address'
build:asan --strip=never
# The instrumentation leaves one common symbol of its own in every object, which
# the check for a symbol defined twice in a static library reads as a
# definition repeated across the archive.
build:asan --features=-symbol_check
# A test of what the compiler costs as it ships -- time, stack, memory -- would
# measure this build instead, so the set is the gate's own less those.
test:asan --test_tag_filters=-nightly,-cost
# A thread's alternate signal stack has one slot and several owners: the
# sanitizer maps one when a thread starts, LLVM's signal handling swaps in a
# larger one of its own from the heap, and the sanitizer runtime of the clang
# and GCC that build Lyra unmaps whichever stack is in the slot when the thread
# ends, which fails on LLVM's and stops the process. The sanitizer of LLVM 23
# unmaps only the one it mapped; until the compilers building Lyra carry that,
# the sanitizer leaves the slot to the program.
test:asan --test_env=ASAN_OPTIONS=use_sigaltstack=0

# Shared cache and result streaming with actions still running locally. Needs a
# BuildBuddy key via `--remote_header`.
build:ci --bes_results_url=https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/
build:ci --bes_backend=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
build:ci --remote_cache=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
build:ci --remote_timeout=10m
build:ci --remote_upload_local_results=true

# Actions run in BuildBuddy's Ubuntu container. Outputs stay remote because a
# compile gate and a remotely executed test never read what they produce, and
# transfer is metered. Needs a BuildBuddy key via `--remote_header`.
build:rbe --remote_executor=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
build:rbe --remote_cache=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io
build:rbe --remote_timeout=10m
build:rbe --remote_download_minimal
build:rbe --extra_execution_platforms=@buildbuddy_toolchain//:platform
build:rbe --host_platform=@buildbuddy_toolchain//:platform
build:rbe --jobs=50
build:rbe --bes_results_url=https://app.buildbuddy.io/invocation/
build:rbe --bes_backend=grpcs://remote.buildbuddy.io

# Remote execution for the edit loop rather than for a gate. The one difference
# is that top-level outputs come back: a loop runs the binary it just built,
# where a gate reads nothing it produces. Concurrency needs no setting here --
# the remote scheduler is a single queue across every invocation, which is the
# coordination that several Bazel servers on one machine cannot have.
build:dev --config=rbe
build:dev --remote_download_toplevel

try-import %workspace%/.bazelrc.user
